01:28:13.727 --> 01:28:15.210
It's like the meme drink.

01:28:15.210 --> 01:28:16.760
- It's like the most
disgusting thing I ever had.

01:28:16.760 --> 01:28:18.307
- Is that like White Claw?

01:28:18.307 --> 01:28:21.760
- No, LaCroix is like, it's like salted?

01:28:21.760 --> 01:28:23.542
- Flavored, sparkling water.

01:28:23.542 --> 01:28:26.270
- It was weird, 'cause I think
you went two years in a row.

01:28:26.270 --> 01:28:28.180
- Yeah, and I didn't go to the one

01:28:28.180 --> 01:28:30.280
where they were streaming
out, the Twitch headquarters.

01:28:30.280 --> 01:28:31.913
Where they streaming
at the Twitch quarters?

01:28:31.913 --> 01:28:32.770
- No, they were streaming in their office.

01:28:32.770 --> 01:28:35.340
- Oh, okay.
- So that was like a back room

01:28:35.340 --> 01:28:39.470
with a small crowd of about
100 people, and it was fine.

01:28:39.470 --> 01:28:42.390
I really got bored of how many
times you had to practice.

01:28:42.390 --> 01:28:43.223
- How many times did you have to rehearse?

01:28:43.223 --> 01:28:44.660
' Cause we only-
- Four times.

01:28:44.660 --> 01:28:45.970
- we only had to rehearse once.

01:28:45.970 --> 01:28:47.960
- Four fucking times.
- Jesus, four?

01:28:47.960 --> 01:28:49.640
- What were they rehearsing?

01:28:49.640 --> 01:28:51.297
- I think it was for the host,

01:28:51.297 --> 01:28:53.150
'cause obviously, he had a lot of lines.

01:28:53.150 --> 01:28:54.583
For me it was easy, I just,

01:28:54.583 --> 01:28:57.160
oh, during the rehearsals,
by the way, I mean gone...

01:28:57.160 --> 01:28:59.470
Okay, so I gotta tell the story.

01:28:59.470 --> 01:29:04.470
So they wanted us to have
a mixture of scripted

01:29:05.580 --> 01:29:08.320
and do your own stuff as I
think it should be, right?

01:29:08.320 --> 01:29:11.850
Like, I want to be on track,
but I also wanna be myself.

01:29:11.850 --> 01:29:13.437
So they would ask me a
question that was kind of,

01:29:13.437 --> 01:29:15.130
"Who do you think is best skilled?"

01:29:15.130 --> 01:29:20.130
And during rehearsals,

01:29:20.586 --> 01:29:23.320
I never said like anything
even remotely Mimi.

01:29:23.320 --> 01:29:25.465
I was like, "Oh yeah, like Rem."

01:29:25.465 --> 01:29:26.732
And then I texted Gigguk like
an hour before the show start,

01:29:26.732 --> 01:29:29.840
I was like, "Yeah, give me the worst girl.

01:29:29.840 --> 01:29:31.440
Give me the worst, best girl,

01:29:31.440 --> 01:29:33.610
And I will dead-ass say
this like straight face

01:29:33.610 --> 01:29:36.410
in front of all these
people, like I'll do it.

01:29:36.410 --> 01:29:37.607
I'll do it on the Twitch
stream, I don't mind."

01:29:37.607 --> 01:29:39.300
'Cause I thought, I wanna make this funny.

01:29:39.300 --> 01:29:41.080
In a good way, that wasn't
fucking with Crunchyroll,

01:29:41.080 --> 01:29:43.780
but there's a way that we could
get a bit of laughter out.

01:29:43.780 --> 01:29:45.997
- I said Nina from "Code Geass",

01:29:45.997 --> 01:29:48.883
that was the first name that came to mind.

01:29:51.070 --> 01:29:51.903
- I remember I said that joke

01:29:51.903 --> 01:29:53.870
and the fucking crowd's like,

01:29:53.870 --> 01:29:55.820
no one got it.
- I watched the stream.

01:29:57.814 --> 01:30:00.713
- I was fucking pissing myself.

01:30:00.713 --> 01:30:01.546
- I thought to myself,

01:30:01.546 --> 01:30:02.980
'cause I knew no one was
gonna laugh at this joke,

01:30:02.980 --> 01:30:04.020
and I just thought to myself,

01:30:04.020 --> 01:30:06.532
I was like, the Twitch
chat, they're gonna love it,

01:30:06.532 --> 01:30:07.560
Twitch chat's gonna love this.

01:30:07.560 --> 01:30:10.240
I don't mind, I'll just put
up with the awkward silence

01:30:10.240 --> 01:30:11.340
for like one second.

01:30:11.340 --> 01:30:12.870
- I was watching the Twitch stream,

01:30:12.870 --> 01:30:15.170
and the moment you said that, I died,

01:30:15.170 --> 01:30:16.670
Twitch chat was fucking loving it,

01:30:16.670 --> 01:30:20.780
- But the crowd in the audience
and everyone's there, dead.

01:30:20.780 --> 01:30:23.310
- I remember the moment I said it,

01:30:23.310 --> 01:30:25.600
for some reason, Jacob
from Fakku was in the crowd

01:30:25.600 --> 01:30:27.550
and I just made eye contact with Jacob

01:30:27.550 --> 01:30:28.750
and he looked like this.

01:30:30.324 --> 01:30:34.020
I was like, and I think I
made like a joke or something,

01:30:34.020 --> 01:30:35.694
I made something to
like try and segway it,

01:30:35.694 --> 01:30:36.990
and it didn't work very well obviously,

01:30:36.990 --> 01:30:38.620
but I didn't give a shit.

01:30:38.620 --> 01:30:39.453
- It made for a great clip

01:30:39.453 --> 01:30:41.585
because it was like so
perfectly timed of my stream,

01:30:41.585 --> 01:30:44.310
'cause I was like, yeah,
I told Connor to say Nina,

01:30:44.310 --> 01:30:46.310
and as soon as I said that, you said it,

01:30:47.162 --> 01:30:49.580
and it was just like the
most perfectly timed moment

01:30:49.580 --> 01:30:51.122
I could ever hope for.

01:30:51.122 --> 01:30:55.331
- Asked me, what he should say for this,

01:30:55.331 --> 01:30:57.960
in the chat, he was like,
"They're gonna ask me

01:30:57.960 --> 01:31:01.978
who the all time best girl is."

01:31:01.978 --> 01:31:03.231
- What'd you tell him?

01:31:03.231 --> 01:31:08.231
- I told him, say Nina from
"Code Geass". (laughing)

01:31:12.661 --> 01:31:13.722
- [Panelist] Connor?

01:31:13.722 --> 01:31:15.407
- Umh, Nina from "Code Geass".

01:31:15.407 --> 01:31:16.407
- Oh my god!

01:31:17.920 --> 01:31:19.380
- Yeah, it was, it was good.

01:31:19.380 --> 01:31:24.380
I mean, it was fun, we
hung out in the green room

01:31:24.460 --> 01:31:27.250
or whatever, it was
chill, just chatting with,

01:31:27.250 --> 01:31:30.553
I remember, I chat with
ProZD, really cool guy.

01:31:31.570 --> 01:31:33.650
That man knows his anime,
he knows a lot of anime.

01:31:33.650 --> 01:31:34.840
- He knows so much anime.

01:31:34.840 --> 01:31:37.430
because I had a similar
conversation with him

01:31:37.430 --> 01:31:40.303
and I'm like, man you know,
more anime the most AniTubers.

01:31:41.330 --> 01:31:43.210
- We were just in this
room and it was silent,

01:31:43.210 --> 01:31:44.200
then you would just hear someone playing

01:31:44.200 --> 01:31:45.150
the fucking "Love Live".

01:31:45.150 --> 01:31:46.900
Just ProZD sitting there like this.

01:31:48.430 --> 01:31:49.552
- He's a Love Liver?

01:31:49.552 --> 01:31:51.930
- He's a hardcore Love Liver.

01:31:51.930 --> 01:31:53.600
- I love it when like, when he would talk,

01:31:53.600 --> 01:31:55.470
'cause it would be like
whenever ProZD talked,

01:31:55.470 --> 01:31:56.303
everyone in the room was like,

01:31:56.303 --> 01:31:57.467
"Yes, ProZD, I'll listen to you."

01:31:57.467 --> 01:32:00.570
He got such a commanding
voice, it's amazing.

01:32:00.570 --> 01:32:01.970
- Who has the deeper voice in real life?

01:32:01.970 --> 01:32:04.590
- It's like just two mumbles
talking when they're talking,

01:32:04.590 --> 01:32:06.500
because he's like, "Get McDonald's,"

01:32:06.500 --> 01:32:08.087
I be, "Yeah, I'll get McDonald's."

01:32:09.700 --> 01:32:12.773
- The subwoofer on this
conversation is ridiculous.

01:32:15.580 --> 01:32:16.413
- Overall it was good.

01:32:16.413 --> 01:32:18.530
I think that the parts I remember the most

01:32:18.530 --> 01:32:20.533
are like not the actual, like awards.

01:32:21.400 --> 01:32:22.630
- Same, I don't remember anything,

01:32:22.630 --> 01:32:24.950
all that happened was
that when I was on stage.

01:32:24.950 --> 01:32:29.296
'Cause I remember when I
was on the Crunchyroll,

01:32:29.296 --> 01:32:30.697
it was a big fucking crowd.

01:32:30.697 --> 01:32:32.220
How big was your crowd?

01:32:32.220 --> 01:32:33.750
- Like no more than 100.

01:32:33.750 --> 01:32:34.810
- Right, 'cause we were like,

01:32:34.810 --> 01:32:36.920
they rented our whole
stage in LA or something.

01:32:36.920 --> 01:32:38.271
They had like a red carpet,

01:32:38.271 --> 01:32:40.063
which like, an orange calm.

01:32:41.090 --> 01:32:42.834
It was charming about like,

01:32:42.834 --> 01:32:44.410
'cause it was still like
a small scale event,

01:32:44.410 --> 01:32:46.040
but they really tried to dress this up

01:32:46.040 --> 01:32:48.130
to look like something official.

01:32:48.130 --> 01:32:50.279
And you know, there was a
lot of technical difficulties

01:32:50.279 --> 01:32:54.040
and mess up, but that's
probably like, you know,

01:32:54.040 --> 01:32:54.873
if you watch the streams-

01:32:54.873 --> 01:32:58.120
- The technical difficulties
in that year were glorious.

01:32:58.120 --> 01:32:59.743
- They made for like great meme material.

01:32:59.743 --> 01:33:01.310
And it was like charming in its own way

01:33:01.310 --> 01:33:03.210
because it wasn't like The Game Awards,

01:33:03.210 --> 01:33:04.360
it wasn't the fucking Oscars,

01:33:04.360 --> 01:33:05.650
but you know, they tried their best.

01:33:05.650 --> 01:33:07.750
And I think for what they had,

01:33:07.750 --> 01:33:09.630
they did like a pretty decent job.

01:33:09.630 --> 01:33:10.650
- B plus for effort, I'd say.

01:33:10.650 --> 01:33:13.370
- I think they said that
they wanted to dial it back

01:33:13.370 --> 01:33:15.410
this year to make it a little more like

01:33:15.410 --> 01:33:17.450
have personality shine through a bit more.

01:33:17.450 --> 01:33:22.070
But it's still hard
because I think sometimes

01:33:22.070 --> 01:33:25.310
some people aren't as
good on stage as others.

01:33:25.310 --> 01:33:28.620
- Is that your first
time kind of "hosting"?

01:33:28.620 --> 01:33:30.300
- I wasn't doing anything?

01:33:30.300 --> 01:33:33.710
- He was literally just sitting
on a couch, cracking jokes.

01:33:33.710 --> 01:33:35.760
- The only thing I was nervous
about was cracking that joke,

01:33:35.760 --> 01:33:38.900
'cause I knew that was gonna,
I knew no one would laugh,

01:33:38.900 --> 01:33:40.010
but I didn't mind.

01:33:40.010 --> 01:33:40.843
But other than that,

01:33:40.843 --> 01:33:42.900
I really don't mind doing
stuff like that, that's fun.

01:33:42.900 --> 01:33:44.670
- I remember the only
weird thing that happened

01:33:44.670 --> 01:33:48.500
when I was on stage was, I
was standing next to Lily

01:33:48.500 --> 01:33:50.080
'cause I was hosting with Lily Puchi

01:33:50.080 --> 01:33:51.540
when I was presenting my award,

01:33:51.540 --> 01:33:54.048
and so I was staying at the bright lights

01:33:54.048 --> 01:33:56.885
and all I heard was, (muffling)

01:33:56.885 --> 01:33:58.950
and I thought someone
said, "I love you guys."

01:33:58.950 --> 01:34:01.017
And I shout out in the Crunchyroll awards,

01:34:01.017 --> 01:34:02.420
"I love you too, man."

01:34:02.420 --> 01:34:03.970
And then I've watched the vods,

01:34:05.108 --> 01:34:09.698
and the guy shouts out, "I
love you, Lily." (laughing)

01:34:09.698 --> 01:34:10.531
- That would be even better.

01:34:10.531 --> 01:34:12.127
I would be like, no, I love you.

01:34:14.207 --> 01:34:16.390
- You may not love me, but
I love you unconditionally.

01:34:16.390 --> 01:34:18.500
- I mean, I don't think
anyone noticed there,

01:34:18.500 --> 01:34:20.130
but yeah, I thought
you were just screaming

01:34:20.130 --> 01:34:21.363
for us both there.

01:34:22.220 --> 01:34:23.950
- When they did the final award,

01:34:23.950 --> 01:34:25.220
at Crunchyroll anime awards,

01:34:25.220 --> 01:34:27.200
they had like a giant,
like what is it called?

01:34:27.200 --> 01:34:29.095
The fucking thing they open, what's it?

01:34:29.095 --> 01:34:29.928
- Sake?

01:34:29.928 --> 01:34:31.450
- No, I think it is, they all had Sake,

01:34:31.450 --> 01:34:32.283
that's part of the story,

01:34:32.283 --> 01:34:33.633
but they have like the
big boxes that we had,

01:34:33.633 --> 01:34:37.020
like the names of the winners in,

01:34:37.020 --> 01:34:38.770
the anime of the year
had like a giant one.

01:34:38.770 --> 01:34:39.653
- Oh yeah.

01:34:41.240 --> 01:34:42.073
I don't know what they're called,

01:34:42.073 --> 01:34:42.906
I know what you're talking about though.

01:34:42.906 --> 01:34:43.850
- So when they did the giant one,

01:34:43.850 --> 01:34:45.750
they were like, "Yeah, okay,
everyone's gonna go on stage

01:34:45.750 --> 01:34:48.430
and we have a fake Sake
for you, it's water."

01:34:48.430 --> 01:34:49.720
And I was like, oh cool, cool, cool.

01:34:49.720 --> 01:34:52.610
So I have it right, and I'm
smelling it like next to Lily.

01:34:52.610 --> 01:34:54.450
I think you can see me
and Lily in the VODs,

01:34:54.450 --> 01:34:55.730
like visual confusion,

01:34:55.730 --> 01:34:58.677
because we do it, I'm like,
"That's fucking Sake."

01:35:00.460 --> 01:35:02.969
They told us it was fucking water!

01:35:02.969 --> 01:35:05.130
Me and Lily are like,
"God that's fucking vile."

01:35:05.130 --> 01:35:07.177
She's like, "Yeah, that's gross."

01:35:08.564 --> 01:35:09.546
They lied to us.

01:35:09.546 --> 01:35:13.130
Crunchyroll, what if I
was allergic to alcohol?

01:35:13.130 --> 01:35:17.316
- They gave you free alcohol
and you didn't even know.

01:35:17.316 --> 01:35:20.080
- 'Cause there was alcohol
right after in the after-party.

01:35:20.080 --> 01:35:22.450
Okay, so we know that you're
now working for Book Walker

01:35:22.450 --> 01:35:25.280
and GeeXPlus and all that,
that's really great Meilyne.

01:35:26.200 --> 01:35:27.637
- Why say it so condescending?

01:35:27.637 --> 01:35:30.690
- Because that's not what
I care about Meilyne,

01:35:30.690 --> 01:35:32.420
I wanna know what you were doing

01:35:32.420 --> 01:35:33.800
when you were younger in Japan.

01:35:33.800 --> 01:35:35.320
What jobs were you doing?

01:35:35.320 --> 01:35:36.920
- We wanna know about your week.

01:35:39.360 --> 01:35:41.523
- What is your path to
getting to where you are now?

01:35:41.523 --> 01:35:43.950
- We want the viewers to recognize

01:35:43.950 --> 01:35:45.880
with your shonen protagonist art,

01:35:45.880 --> 01:35:48.126
we need to build up Meilyne's character.

01:35:48.126 --> 01:35:50.800
- We've told all of our back stories,

01:35:50.800 --> 01:35:52.273
it's time for us to tell your back story.

01:35:52.273 --> 01:35:53.660
- Backstory? Okay.

01:35:53.660 --> 01:35:57.760
So I used to go to a lot
of anime conventions-

01:35:57.760 --> 01:35:58.920
- As you do.
- Yes,

01:35:58.920 --> 01:36:01.760
and, where am I going with this?

01:36:01.760 --> 01:36:03.040
- That's how you met your husband right?

01:36:03.040 --> 01:36:05.080
- Yeah, yeah, that's the thing.

01:36:05.080 --> 01:36:07.410
I forgot to tell the story
or not tell the story.

01:36:07.410 --> 01:36:08.729
I wanted to say like the biggest tip

01:36:08.729 --> 01:36:13.729
is to envision what position
you want in the anime industry

01:36:13.860 --> 01:36:15.897
and make like steps to get there.

01:36:15.897 --> 01:36:17.980
And the steps I made for myself

01:36:17.980 --> 01:36:20.290
is one, keep going to a
lot of anime conventions

01:36:20.290 --> 01:36:25.217
and make friends that work in some, like-

01:36:25.217 --> 01:36:27.403
- In the industry.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:36:27.403 --> 01:36:31.120
And one of the people
that I met at a convention

01:36:31.120 --> 01:36:35.383
happened to bring Japanese rock bands,

01:36:36.480 --> 01:36:40.040
yeah, Japanese rock bands to
the U.S anime conventions.

01:36:40.040 --> 01:36:44.610
And then that person introduced
me to a lot of people

01:36:44.610 --> 01:36:49.610
that were in Japan and the
hardest thing for a foreigner,

01:36:50.306 --> 01:36:52.620
for someone to get there...

01:36:52.620 --> 01:36:54.080
No, sorry, the hardest thing

01:36:54.080 --> 01:36:57.090
is to get into Japan with the visa.

01:36:57.090 --> 01:37:00.120
Finding a job that'll give you a visa

01:37:00.120 --> 01:37:04.240
when you don't speak good
Japanese is extremely difficult.

01:37:04.240 --> 01:37:08.460
So the first thing I did was get a job

01:37:08.460 --> 01:37:12.810
that has no relation to
anime, to get the experience

01:37:12.810 --> 01:37:16.180
and then try to do a consulting job

01:37:16.180 --> 01:37:21.180
that consults for anime figure,
like, type of companies.

01:37:21.500 --> 01:37:25.920
And then, after that, meet
people from that industry,

01:37:25.920 --> 01:37:28.030
get the know-how from them

01:37:28.030 --> 01:37:31.830
and then apply to the job that I have now.

01:37:31.830 --> 01:37:34.696
But, gosh, I'm like-
- Damn, that's calculated.

01:37:34.696 --> 01:37:35.840
- Yeah, I calculated everything.

01:37:35.840 --> 01:37:37.730
- At the end of the day,
you know, it's that age old,

01:37:37.730 --> 01:37:40.030
like, it's not what you
know, it's who you know.

01:37:41.210 --> 01:37:43.800
- I think I've heard similar
stories with other friends,

01:37:43.800 --> 01:37:47.870
like Nabi, who I had no idea
how he knew so many people

01:37:47.870 --> 01:37:48.703
in the industry.

01:37:48.703 --> 01:37:50.187
And I remember asking
him and he was just like,

01:37:50.187 --> 01:37:51.570
"Yeah, just go to enough events,

01:37:51.570 --> 01:37:52.746
just talk to enough people."

01:37:52.746 --> 01:37:54.170
- There's a lot of events here.

01:37:54.170 --> 01:37:56.543
For me I'm like a nervous wreck.

01:37:57.410 --> 01:37:59.918
- Yeah, we could tell. (laughing)

01:37:59.918 --> 01:38:02.640
- Do not be fooled by
Meilyne's nervousness,

01:38:02.640 --> 01:38:03.850
she is a killer.
(dramatic music)

01:38:03.850 --> 01:38:05.050
- It's only you guys in the room,

01:38:05.050 --> 01:38:06.060
so that's why I'm comfortable,

01:38:06.060 --> 01:38:07.630
but it's like, if there's more than that,

01:38:07.630 --> 01:38:12.630
holy, oh my god, this
would be soaked. (laughing)

01:38:13.140 --> 01:38:15.443
So like you have to meet people, network.

01:38:15.443 --> 01:38:19.080
- And a lot of people will
find that difficult in the U.S

01:38:19.080 --> 01:38:21.580
and abroad, 'cause the only
place I could possibly think of

01:38:21.580 --> 01:38:23.200
is like conventions and everything.

01:38:23.200 --> 01:38:25.707
But in Japan, there are
so many official events

01:38:25.707 --> 01:38:27.803
that you can go to to meet people.

01:38:27.803 --> 01:38:29.700
What would you recommend then?

01:38:29.700 --> 01:38:31.370
Like what should people look out for

01:38:31.370 --> 01:38:33.740
if they wanted to just
be in the right place

01:38:33.740 --> 01:38:35.000
at the right time?

01:38:35.000 --> 01:38:37.260
- There's a lot of
Facebook groups actually,

01:38:37.260 --> 01:38:39.230
and a lot of these Facebook groups

01:38:39.230 --> 01:38:40.820
are for like the otaku community,

01:38:40.820 --> 01:38:42.980
and sometimes they have industry people

01:38:42.980 --> 01:38:45.370
as surprise guests or like just show up.

01:38:45.370 --> 01:38:47.310
So go to those and you can meet people,

01:38:47.310 --> 01:38:50.860
or when there's like,
they're kinda like panels,

01:38:50.860 --> 01:38:52.270
industry panels,

01:38:52.270 --> 01:38:55.933
you can meet people at the
after party of these things.

01:38:58.460 --> 01:39:00.640
- From experience, I've had people,

01:39:00.640 --> 01:39:03.144
one thing I know that doesn't work

01:39:03.144 --> 01:39:07.380
is to just go up to someone

01:39:07.380 --> 01:39:09.620
and being like, "Hey, I'm this person,

01:39:09.620 --> 01:39:11.370
here's my business card, call me."

01:39:11.370 --> 01:39:15.133
And I'm just like, I don't
know what you do bro.

01:39:16.159 --> 01:39:17.723
What do you want to do with me?

01:39:18.650 --> 01:39:19.650
- I've had that so much.

01:39:19.650 --> 01:39:22.820
They come to you really like
suave, like, "Here's my card,

01:39:22.820 --> 01:39:24.160
I expect to hear from you."

01:39:24.160 --> 01:39:25.733
- You small talk first though.

01:39:26.896 --> 01:39:29.700
- At least explain what is the purpose

01:39:29.700 --> 01:39:32.020
of you going up to people, right?

01:39:32.020 --> 01:39:34.240
Like, what is the kind of
connection that you want?

01:39:34.240 --> 01:39:36.920
It's not just about like,
"I wanna make a connection

01:39:36.920 --> 01:39:38.010
because I want a connection."

01:39:38.010 --> 01:39:40.030
It's about, why do you
want the connection,

01:39:40.030 --> 01:39:41.360
what do you wanna do with the connection

01:39:41.360 --> 01:39:44.420
and explain why it'll be
mutually beneficial, right?

01:39:44.420 --> 01:39:46.870
They'll just be like, "Here's
my business card, call me."

01:39:46.870 --> 01:39:49.950
- If you know that they would
be in a beneficial position

01:39:49.950 --> 01:39:50.903
sometimes I might just play dumb

01:39:50.903 --> 01:39:52.570
and be like, "Oh yes, what do you do?"

01:39:52.570 --> 01:39:53.403
- They'll tell me and I'll be like,

01:39:53.403 --> 01:39:55.989
"Oh, no way, I'm in the same industry."

01:39:55.989 --> 01:39:57.536
But then they're like,
yeah, that's organic, right?

01:39:57.536 --> 01:39:58.993
But the whole time, I'll be like-

01:40:00.120 --> 01:40:03.013
(Joey laughing)

01:40:03.013 --> 01:40:05.170
- Because a lot of weebs don't realize

01:40:05.170 --> 01:40:07.750
that one of the most important
skills you need to learn

01:40:07.750 --> 01:40:10.938
when getting a job in any
industry, it's just social skills.

01:40:10.938 --> 01:40:13.257
- God-dam it, no!

01:40:13.257 --> 01:40:16.590
- Not a lot, you just
gotta learn social skills.

01:40:16.590 --> 01:40:18.520
- But I don't wanna talk to people!

01:40:18.520 --> 01:40:22.640
- I actually tutored random
people in English, in Japan

01:40:22.640 --> 01:40:24.200
to develop social skills.

01:40:24.200 --> 01:40:29.010
- And like, it should be
inspiration for all of you.

01:40:29.010 --> 01:40:32.235
If this nervous wreck can
develop enough social skills

01:40:32.235 --> 01:40:33.380
to be working in the industry

01:40:33.380 --> 01:40:35.060
and convince fucking Japanese companies

01:40:35.060 --> 01:40:38.453
to form GeeXPlus, there's
hope for you as well, okay?

01:40:41.883 --> 01:40:44.140
- No amount of social anxiety
is gonna stop you, alright?

01:40:44.140 --> 01:40:45.049
- 'Cause the first time I called you,

01:40:45.049 --> 01:40:47.060
I was like, "Wow, why is
she so fucking nervous?"

01:40:47.060 --> 01:40:48.337
- On the phone, I think you were like,

01:40:48.337 --> 01:40:49.530
"Oh, it's okay, don't be nervous."

01:40:49.530 --> 01:40:52.720
And I'm like, "Oh it's just,
it's winter, it's really cold."

01:40:52.720 --> 01:40:53.660
'Cause my voice gets shaking.

01:40:53.660 --> 01:40:55.230
- That's the worst thing
to say though, right?

01:40:55.230 --> 01:40:56.884
- Yeah, and it got me more nervous.

01:40:56.884 --> 01:40:57.717
- Saying "Don't be nervous,"

01:40:57.717 --> 01:40:58.986
it's just gonna get them more nervous.

01:40:58.986 --> 01:41:00.813
- I think I was like,
"Why are you so nervous?"

01:41:00.813 --> 01:41:01.646
- Yeah, you said that,

01:41:01.646 --> 01:41:04.620
and I was like, "No,
it's cold, it's cold."

01:41:04.620 --> 01:41:07.197
- I didn't know that was like-
- That's a no-no.

01:41:09.640 --> 01:41:11.700
- I wanted to see what kind of
reaction Meilyne would give,

01:41:11.700 --> 01:41:12.533
I don't know why.

01:41:12.533 --> 01:41:14.640
I just wanted to see,
'cause I just got a vibe

01:41:14.640 --> 01:41:15.473
that you would react really funny.

01:41:15.473 --> 01:41:16.306
- We met in real life first,

01:41:16.306 --> 01:41:17.530
at the anime expo.
- We did,

01:41:17.530 --> 01:41:18.900
that's why I didn't mind.
- And then you said

01:41:18.900 --> 01:41:19.837
the same thing, you were like,

01:41:19.837 --> 01:41:21.910
"Oh, you don't have to be so awkward."

01:41:21.910 --> 01:41:22.743
- 'Cause we were talking

01:41:22.743 --> 01:41:25.480
and you're like, "Well, I
don't know if we can, um,"

01:41:25.480 --> 01:41:29.063
and I was like, what the fuck's
wrong with you? (laughing)

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:31.840
- Cause I remember my
first business meeting

01:41:31.840 --> 01:41:35.340
with you as well and whenever
Meilyne's negotiating,

01:41:35.340 --> 01:41:38.050
it's like the most hilarious
thing I've ever seen.

01:41:38.050 --> 01:41:39.640
- When we had the piece of paper out,

01:41:39.640 --> 01:41:41.993
and I was like, "How about this?"

01:41:43.640 --> 01:41:45.820
- I feel like it's so obvious

01:41:45.820 --> 01:41:47.642
if you don't like the deal that's offered.

01:41:47.642 --> 01:41:48.792
You're like, (humming).

01:41:49.830 --> 01:41:52.290
- It's like, "How do I say no

01:41:52.290 --> 01:41:55.220
without sounding like an asshole?"

01:41:55.220 --> 01:41:59.397
- I know how she says it, she says, "Oh."

01:42:01.330 --> 01:42:02.917
When you hear Meilyne saying that,

01:42:02.917 --> 01:42:05.907
you're immediately like,
"Oh, it's not good, is it?"

01:42:07.183 --> 01:42:09.410
- I was about to say, you can
write a fuckin' dictionary

01:42:09.410 --> 01:42:12.730
on your fuckin' ohs, and
like, which tone you say it in

01:42:12.730 --> 01:42:14.800
just communicates something
completely different.

01:42:14.800 --> 01:42:16.930
- You even type it to us as well.

01:42:16.930 --> 01:42:18.710
When I'm texting you about a business deal

01:42:18.710 --> 01:42:19.563
and I'm like, "Oh they
came back with this,

01:42:19.563 --> 01:42:20.640
what do you think?"

01:42:20.640 --> 01:42:24.019
And you legitimately come back with, "Oh,"

01:42:24.019 --> 01:42:26.249
and immediately I'm
just like, "Say no more,

01:42:26.249 --> 01:42:27.520
I know exactly what you mean."

01:42:27.520 --> 01:42:29.370
- I remembered a story, I
don't know if I've told this.

01:42:29.370 --> 01:42:32.660
I told you guys about the time
at "Promare" there was a guy?

01:42:32.660 --> 01:42:33.493
- No, no.

01:42:33.493 --> 01:42:35.523
- So, when I was at the
premier of "Promare",

01:42:35.523 --> 01:42:38.150
I was sitting in the Studio Trigger seats

01:42:38.150 --> 01:42:41.830
'cause a friend got me into
the Studio Trigger seats

01:42:41.830 --> 01:42:44.080
and I was like, well, it's fuckin' cool.

01:42:44.080 --> 01:42:46.523
So I'm sitting on the end
of the Studio Trigger seats,

01:42:46.523 --> 01:42:47.397
"Promare" is about to start,

01:42:47.397 --> 01:42:49.709
and it's obviously Studio Trigger seats

01:42:49.709 --> 01:42:51.270
'cause all the seats say
reserved for Studio Trigger.

01:42:51.270 --> 01:42:54.743
So I'm sitting there, and this
guy in like a final fantasy

01:42:54.743 --> 01:42:56.427
like Claude cosplay comes up to me

01:42:56.427 --> 01:42:59.430
and he goes, "Hey do you work at Trigger?"

01:42:59.430 --> 01:43:02.330
And I was like, no, these guys do.

01:43:02.330 --> 01:43:04.013
He's like, and then I say that to him

01:43:04.013 --> 01:43:06.110
and was like, Oh I've made a mistake,

01:43:06.110 --> 01:43:07.940
'cause then he's like, "Oh, hello."

01:43:07.940 --> 01:43:09.440
And then I'm like, Oh my god."

01:43:10.550 --> 01:43:12.190
Over me, he's like, "Hello?"

01:43:12.190 --> 01:43:15.003
Bear in mind, he's full
costume, fuckin' giant sword.

01:43:16.050 --> 01:43:17.820
He's like, "How do I get
a job at Studio Trigger?"

01:43:17.820 --> 01:43:20.140
to this Japanese man who
can't speak a word of English.

01:43:20.140 --> 01:43:22.620
And he's like (sound)

01:43:24.782 --> 01:43:26.950
- Yeah, you should've just
been like, "Not like this."

01:43:26.950 --> 01:43:27.929
- Literally, I was like,

01:43:27.929 --> 01:43:28.940
"Bro, I don't think
this is the right time."

01:43:28.940 --> 01:43:31.090
He asked that to them,
they were visibly confused,

01:43:31.090 --> 01:43:33.500
and I'm like, "Bro, I don't
think this is the right play."

01:43:33.500 --> 01:43:34.400
He's like, "Oh, okay."

01:43:34.400 --> 01:43:37.210
And I'm like, what The fuck?

01:43:37.210 --> 01:43:38.310
- With these kinds of people,

01:43:38.310 --> 01:43:40.400
what do they expect the answer to be?

01:43:40.400 --> 01:43:43.150
Be like, "Oh yes, here's my
business card, contact me."

01:43:44.105 --> 01:43:46.720
- It's like, "You were the right guy.

01:43:46.720 --> 01:43:48.740
We were waiting for
someone to be bold enough

01:43:48.740 --> 01:43:51.188
to ask us at the 'Promare'."

01:43:51.188 --> 01:43:52.550
(laughing) Like, no, fuck off!

01:43:52.550 --> 01:43:54.820
- Like no, that's not how you get a job.

01:43:54.820 --> 01:43:58.350
- It's just like one of those
moments, I was like, "Oh no."

01:43:58.350 --> 01:44:00.400
- That's secondhand embarrassment, right?

01:44:01.280 --> 01:44:04.950
- This is the biggest L
I've ever seen in my life.

01:44:04.950 --> 01:44:07.300
- So, advice, at your next convention

01:44:07.300 --> 01:44:10.350
and you see an industry
guest, don't do that.

01:44:10.350 --> 01:44:11.470
That's not how you get a job.

01:44:11.470 --> 01:44:12.976
- I couldn't believe it.

01:44:12.976 --> 01:44:14.180
- I don't think they're looking to hire

01:44:14.180 --> 01:44:16.356
when they're at conventions.

01:44:16.356 --> 01:44:18.550
- The person that's
like, the hiring person

01:44:18.550 --> 01:44:21.000
wouldn't go to that
event anyways, I think.

01:44:21.000 --> 01:44:23.300
- I mean, if I ran an anime company,

01:44:23.300 --> 01:44:26.050
the last place I would go to hire people

01:44:26.050 --> 01:44:28.170
is the anime convention.

01:44:28.170 --> 01:44:29.970
- It just seems like the way to get hired

01:44:29.970 --> 01:44:33.850
in Japanese companies it's just
first to be in Japan, right?

01:44:33.850 --> 01:44:37.040
- In Japan or like apply to U.S companies,

01:44:37.040 --> 01:44:38.480
like Crunchyroll and Viz Media.

01:44:38.480 --> 01:44:40.910
- Being outright and so like, not even,

01:44:40.910 --> 01:44:43.020
I mean, that's a little
aggressive asking, right?

01:44:43.020 --> 01:44:45.170
Like that kind of way, just doesn't work.

01:44:45.170 --> 01:44:47.090
It's like, you gotta insert yourself.

01:44:47.090 --> 01:44:48.810
- You weren't even cosplaying
as a Trigger character,

01:44:48.810 --> 01:44:51.480
you're cosplay is fuckin' Claude.

01:44:51.480 --> 01:44:54.567
- So if he turned up full,
like real "Kill la Kill".

01:44:55.580 --> 01:44:59.520
- As you can see I am a
fan, can I have a job?

01:44:59.520 --> 01:45:01.850
- It's like our audience
knows what we think

01:45:01.850 --> 01:45:03.620
about working in Japanese companies.

01:45:03.620 --> 01:45:06.970
How has it for you who actually
works in a Japanese company

01:45:06.970 --> 01:45:07.840
for the industry?

01:45:07.840 --> 01:45:09.060
- I worked in different Japanese companies

01:45:09.060 --> 01:45:13.763
before the anime one, awkward mode on.

01:45:13.763 --> 01:45:16.263
- It's okay, it won't hurt
you, it's a microphone.

01:45:19.644 --> 01:45:21.890
- I don't know, you want me
to say it's like exciting,

01:45:21.890 --> 01:45:23.837
it's like nervous.
- No, just be honest.

01:45:23.837 --> 01:45:27.400
- Are the rumors true, are
Japanese companies hell?

01:45:27.400 --> 01:45:28.233
I'm joking.

01:45:29.968 --> 01:45:32.170
'Cause just like black companies, white.

01:45:32.170 --> 01:45:34.740
- Yeah, so like, Japan
has like this terminology

01:45:34.740 --> 01:45:36.930
where it's like, it's black or it's white.

01:45:36.930 --> 01:45:38.420
White is like, they follow the rules,

01:45:38.420 --> 01:45:39.400
they treat their employees nice,

01:45:39.400 --> 01:45:41.470
and then black is like, they overwork you,

01:45:41.470 --> 01:45:43.546
they ask you to work like, I don't know,

01:45:43.546 --> 01:45:46.700
12 hours or something like that.

01:45:46.700 --> 01:45:49.470
But no, I got really lucky, I think.

01:45:49.470 --> 01:45:54.470
The company I work for is
really like very aggressive

01:45:55.050 --> 01:45:56.187
about telling their employees,

01:45:56.187 --> 01:45:58.690
"Go home, have a social life."

01:45:58.690 --> 01:46:03.360
And they highly, highly value that.

01:46:03.360 --> 01:46:04.193
- That's good.
- Well, what do you think

01:46:04.193 --> 01:46:07.173
about the Japanese like
work-life schedule?

01:46:08.350 --> 01:46:09.391
- As someone who lives there.

01:46:09.391 --> 01:46:11.386
- I think it's on average Japanese,

01:46:11.386 --> 01:46:14.030
they work on average 80 hours a week.

01:46:14.030 --> 01:46:15.419
- Yeah.
- Jesus Christ.

01:46:15.419 --> 01:46:16.470
- 80 hours a week.

01:46:16.470 --> 01:46:18.210
- How was like your work-life schedule

01:46:18.210 --> 01:46:21.100
compared to when you are
working outside of Japan,

01:46:21.100 --> 01:46:22.890
if you have any comparison.

01:46:22.890 --> 01:46:25.030
- Well, I only worked
for an American company

01:46:25.030 --> 01:46:28.860
in like San Francisco
for a month before I quit

01:46:28.860 --> 01:46:30.293
and chase my anime dreams.

01:46:33.150 --> 01:46:35.760
I guess, based on that
one month experience,

01:46:35.760 --> 01:46:36.740
it's very different.

01:46:36.740 --> 01:46:39.040
Like, in America I would just clock out

01:46:39.040 --> 01:46:40.940
and leave that's it,

01:46:40.940 --> 01:46:45.430
Here, it's like, you wanna
impress your supervisor

01:46:45.430 --> 01:46:48.980
with good results and sometimes
to get those good results,

01:46:48.980 --> 01:46:50.680
I mean, it doesn't
necessarily make more time

01:46:50.680 --> 01:46:53.290
but if you spend more time
doing more research...

01:46:53.290 --> 01:46:56.553
- Meilyne's a hard worker.
- I don't know, maybe.

01:46:57.560 --> 01:46:58.550
- On the polar opposite,

01:46:58.550 --> 01:47:00.150
you got like the Scandinavian countries

01:47:00.150 --> 01:47:02.490
which are like six hours a day is fine,

01:47:02.490 --> 01:47:04.250
four days a week, that seems good.

01:47:04.250 --> 01:47:05.840
But I mean, there's been plenty of studies

01:47:05.840 --> 01:47:07.490
that prove that working more

01:47:07.490 --> 01:47:09.780
doesn't necessarily mean more money.

01:47:09.780 --> 01:47:11.005
I mean, there's studies to actually prove

01:47:11.005 --> 01:47:13.389
the opposite of that,
which is working less

01:47:13.389 --> 01:47:14.222
tends to be more productive.

01:47:14.222 --> 01:47:15.510
- I mean, that's where
the whole controversy

01:47:15.510 --> 01:47:18.430
of the whole thing comes from, right?

01:47:18.430 --> 01:47:21.440
Where like employees of
traditional companies

01:47:21.440 --> 01:47:24.020
are almost forced, like socially,

01:47:24.020 --> 01:47:25.240
it's not a rule in the company,

01:47:25.240 --> 01:47:28.970
but they're almost socially
bound to stay back overtime

01:47:28.970 --> 01:47:29.803
to work.

01:47:29.803 --> 01:47:30.636
- I don't think they should though.

01:47:30.636 --> 01:47:32.760
- Not because they need to do the work,

01:47:32.760 --> 01:47:34.300
but because as you said,

01:47:34.300 --> 01:47:36.540
they wanna impress the supervisors, right?

01:47:36.540 --> 01:47:37.453
- Yeah, kind of.

01:47:38.607 --> 01:47:40.920
But there are some times,

01:47:40.920 --> 01:47:43.930
I think they really shouldn't stay behind

01:47:43.930 --> 01:47:46.370
even if they're not really
"working" or working

01:47:46.370 --> 01:47:48.880
because it really takes a
toll on their mental health.

01:47:48.880 --> 01:47:50.790
Like for me, usually before an event

01:47:50.790 --> 01:47:52.350
the month or month and a half,

01:47:52.350 --> 01:47:53.683
I'm actually working like really late,

01:47:53.683 --> 01:47:55.610
like 11:00 PM or something

01:47:55.610 --> 01:47:58.180
because crunch time type of thing.

01:47:58.180 --> 01:48:01.860
And I felt like, miserable
during those times,

01:48:01.860 --> 01:48:06.040
so I can only imagine how
miserable Japanese people

01:48:06.040 --> 01:48:09.180
at Japanese companies that do overtime-

01:48:09.180 --> 01:48:10.013
- I feel sorry for them.

01:48:10.013 --> 01:48:11.510
- Yeah. I felt angry for no reason.

01:48:11.510 --> 01:48:12.343
I'm like, why am I angry?

01:48:12.343 --> 01:48:14.100
Oh, it's because, am I working too much?

01:48:14.100 --> 01:48:15.603
But I really loved what I did.

01:48:18.040 --> 01:48:19.750
Even if you love what you do though,

01:48:19.750 --> 01:48:22.180
when you get tired, you get cranky.

01:48:22.180 --> 01:48:23.340
- I mean, that's the same as us.

01:48:23.340 --> 01:48:26.730
I mean, we love our jobs
but we're always working,

01:48:26.730 --> 01:48:30.600
and we never get a break but
even though we love what we do

01:48:30.600 --> 01:48:32.910
there's times when I fuckin' hate anime.

01:48:32.910 --> 01:48:33.743
You're just like, no.

01:48:33.743 --> 01:48:35.147
- There are times where I'm just like,

01:48:35.147 --> 01:48:37.040
"I don't wanna work on this video man,

01:48:37.040 --> 01:48:37.873
I just wanna play games."

01:48:37.873 --> 01:48:39.413
- I know, I know.
- Watch porn or something.

01:48:39.413 --> 01:48:42.463
it's like anything else to
just get my mind off of it.

01:48:43.567 --> 01:48:45.039
- Two hour porn marathon.

01:48:45.039 --> 01:48:48.355
- Just watch it. (laughing)

01:48:48.355 --> 01:48:50.380
It's just one of those days, you know?

01:48:50.380 --> 01:48:52.730
- What's the convention
time like for you then?

01:48:52.730 --> 01:48:55.680
Because for us, it's like,
especially AX is busy as hell

01:48:55.680 --> 01:48:57.790
but I've seen people
working in the industry

01:48:57.790 --> 01:49:00.700
working fucking overtime when
it comes to convention season.

01:49:00.700 --> 01:49:02.577
- I wonder as well, expanding on that is,

01:49:02.577 --> 01:49:06.343
how does it all work with
industries and the conventions?

01:49:07.220 --> 01:49:11.750
- I can only speak from my
particular perspective or spot.

01:49:11.750 --> 01:49:14.500
- I wouldn't expect you to
be the arbiter of knowledge

01:49:14.500 --> 01:49:16.230
on conventions. (laughing)

01:49:16.230 --> 01:49:19.630
- Japanese companies, I
think they have to prepare

01:49:19.630 --> 01:49:21.400
a lot more than U.S companies

01:49:21.400 --> 01:49:23.940
because we have to consider

01:49:23.940 --> 01:49:26.330
people that don't speak
English coming over

01:49:26.330 --> 01:49:30.480
and their first time
exhibiting at an event,

01:49:30.480 --> 01:49:35.480
and the amount of time it
takes to ship something.

01:49:35.663 --> 01:49:37.170
Like, if they're going to sell something,

01:49:37.170 --> 01:49:39.740
at overseas at the convention,

01:49:39.740 --> 01:49:41.410
shipping it might be a problem.

01:49:41.410 --> 01:49:44.690
So it takes like a lot of time.

01:49:44.690 --> 01:49:47.440
- To be fair though,
if I can say something,

01:49:47.440 --> 01:49:49.550
I don't know if it's just Kadokawa,

01:49:49.550 --> 01:49:52.140
I've noticed with any booth

01:49:52.140 --> 01:49:54.530
that is run by a Japanese company,

01:49:54.530 --> 01:49:58.759
the amount of staff they
have, it's kinda ridiculous.

01:49:58.759 --> 01:50:00.853
- There's usually like a supervisor

01:50:00.853 --> 01:50:02.260
that's watching another supervisor

01:50:02.260 --> 01:50:03.583
watching other supervisors.

01:50:05.177 --> 01:50:08.243
- It's like the entire supply
chain watching each other.

01:50:09.150 --> 01:50:10.950
- In American company booths,

01:50:10.950 --> 01:50:14.480
maybe it's like two to five max.

01:50:14.480 --> 01:50:17.240
The Kadokawa booth had like 60 people.

01:50:17.240 --> 01:50:18.910
- I remember going inside
the Kadokawa booth,

01:50:18.910 --> 01:50:20.870
I remember there was like 30 employees.

01:50:20.870 --> 01:50:22.503
I was like, this is just stupid.

01:50:24.010 --> 01:50:27.710
- The reason is, each person,
like, even within Kadokawa,

01:50:27.710 --> 01:50:29.610
say these are all from the same series

01:50:29.610 --> 01:50:31.870
or no, not the same
series, same publisher,

01:50:31.870 --> 01:50:34.370
each department has a
different person in charge

01:50:34.370 --> 01:50:36.843
and that person charged
needs to be there to like-

01:50:36.843 --> 01:50:38.250
- But this feels like it's been like-

01:50:38.250 --> 01:50:39.648
- But do they though?

01:50:39.648 --> 01:50:41.798
- This feels like this
should've been like-

01:50:42.680 --> 01:50:44.047
- Coordinated, right?

01:50:46.066 --> 01:50:47.210
Just get like one person

01:50:47.210 --> 01:50:49.150
to just kind of look over everything

01:50:49.150 --> 01:50:51.680
and then once the event
is done, just report that.

01:50:51.680 --> 01:50:53.623
- Seems like a lot of redundancy.

01:50:53.623 --> 01:50:55.230
- I think it's also important
for them to go overseas

01:50:55.230 --> 01:50:58.500
because they're like, their mind is open,

01:50:59.358 --> 01:51:00.778
you become open-minded?

01:51:00.778 --> 01:51:02.158
- Yeah, sometimes.

01:51:02.158 --> 01:51:05.250
- It's just like how American otakus are.

01:51:05.250 --> 01:51:06.650
- But the problem is, half the employees

01:51:06.650 --> 01:51:08.000
don't come out of the booth.

01:51:08.000 --> 01:51:09.477
So they don't even get to
experience the culture.

01:51:09.477 --> 01:51:13.070
- But they can see, or
hear from outside the booth

01:51:13.070 --> 01:51:15.960
like what item's being sold
the most and like take men-

01:51:15.960 --> 01:51:18.607
- So what do they think
of foreign conventions

01:51:18.607 --> 01:51:22.740
and the foreign culture
then, from your perspective?

01:51:22.740 --> 01:51:24.950
- I've heard think it's
like, wow, amazing.

01:51:24.950 --> 01:51:27.650
So different and like, so open

01:51:27.650 --> 01:51:29.830
compared to the fan otaku culture here.

01:51:29.830 --> 01:51:32.097
- That's what I've heard from
a lot of Japanese people.

01:51:32.097 --> 01:51:34.000
- The fans here are really uptight.

01:51:34.000 --> 01:51:38.190
Like for example, like
Comiket or anime events here,

01:51:38.190 --> 01:51:39.720
you can't go there and cosplay

01:51:39.720 --> 01:51:41.870
because you would bring
shame to the event.

01:51:43.236 --> 01:51:44.990
- Oh yeah, I've heard that story.

01:51:44.990 --> 01:51:48.390
- Actually, my friends
and I cosplayed at Comiket

01:51:48.390 --> 01:51:49.530
like six years ago,

01:51:49.530 --> 01:51:53.590
and we were the top
trending thing at that time

01:51:53.590 --> 01:51:56.680
on tuchan, saying "These
three American girls

01:51:56.680 --> 01:51:58.750
cosplaying as "Love Live" on the train."

01:51:58.750 --> 01:52:02.320
And shame on me for not
doing my research beforehand-

01:52:02.320 --> 01:52:04.310
- But still, chill.
- Yeah.

01:52:04.310 --> 01:52:05.630
I had like friends in America,

01:52:05.630 --> 01:52:06.857
Japanese friends in America messaged me,

01:52:06.857 --> 01:52:08.710
"What are you guys doing over there?

01:52:08.710 --> 01:52:10.310
You can't cosplay in the train."

01:52:11.206 --> 01:52:12.039
- Isn't it fun though?

01:52:12.039 --> 01:52:13.210
I'd love to see a cosplay on the train.

01:52:13.210 --> 01:52:17.183
- We had like a circle follow
us from station to station.

01:52:18.080 --> 01:52:19.650
They were like, "Oh,
can you take that off?"

01:52:19.650 --> 01:52:21.113
But we were only wearing the wig.

01:52:21.113 --> 01:52:23.210
Like I was wearing normal
clothes, just the wig.

01:52:23.210 --> 01:52:25.010
- So you're not even
allowed to wear a wig.

01:52:25.010 --> 01:52:27.033
- Yeah, and then my friend told her,

01:52:27.033 --> 01:52:28.523
like, "You know, we're
only wearing the wig,"

01:52:28.523 --> 01:52:31.040
they're like, "No, they
know from your hair,

01:52:31.040 --> 01:52:32.367
what character you are."

01:52:33.340 --> 01:52:36.410
- But look, I understand if
it's something gratuitous,

01:52:36.410 --> 01:52:39.703
like if you were all
cosplaying as fuckin' Ryuko,

01:52:40.900 --> 01:52:44.707
where it's like a little
more like scandalous right?

01:52:44.707 --> 01:52:47.860
But like, it's "Love Live",
you're in school uniform.

01:52:47.860 --> 01:52:50.820
The only thing that separates
you from a regular school girl

01:52:50.820 --> 01:52:52.160
is just the wig.

01:52:52.160 --> 01:52:54.450
- But to be fair, they
wanna protect this culture,

01:52:54.450 --> 01:52:56.240
they wanna keep having these events.

01:52:56.240 --> 01:52:58.740
- It's just gate keeping.
- That is gate keeping.

01:53:00.830 --> 01:53:03.340
- For example a normie
would be on the train

01:53:03.340 --> 01:53:05.440
and they see some crazy cosplayer.

01:53:05.440 --> 01:53:06.850
- Isn't it fun though?

01:53:08.520 --> 01:53:10.470
- But also like anime otakus in Japan

01:53:10.470 --> 01:53:12.744
aren't viewed in like a positive light.

01:53:12.744 --> 01:53:16.200
- I don't think they necessarily
are though in the West.

01:53:16.200 --> 01:53:18.130
- I don't feel like
there's as bad a stigma

01:53:18.130 --> 01:53:20.260
compared to this here.

01:53:20.260 --> 01:53:23.540
- They think you're like
sweaty and I mean, I am,

01:53:23.540 --> 01:53:26.917
but like, sweaty (laughing).

01:53:28.620 --> 01:53:30.790
You're just so weird,
you watch the weird stuff

01:53:30.790 --> 01:53:31.674
on the internet.

01:53:31.674 --> 01:53:33.880
- Cosplay's not even that weird.

01:53:33.880 --> 01:53:37.180
- I rocked up on the London
underground in a maid outfit.

01:53:37.180 --> 01:53:40.633
Like, just did it, just like
40 minute commute, hell yeah.

01:53:41.880 --> 01:53:43.410
Everyone loves it,
they're like all laughing,

01:53:43.410 --> 01:53:44.487
all giggling and asking questions.

01:53:44.487 --> 01:53:45.550
- Were they being like,
"What do you cosplaying

01:53:45.550 --> 01:53:46.830
or dressing up as?"

01:53:46.830 --> 01:53:49.860
- I get heckled from the
other side of the thing,

01:53:49.860 --> 01:53:51.290
and then like, you know,
it's just good fun.

01:53:51.290 --> 01:53:52.610
I don't know, I thought it was funny,

01:53:52.610 --> 01:53:53.520
but obviously, I guess-

01:53:53.520 --> 01:53:54.910
- It's such a weird double
standard though, isn't it?

01:53:54.910 --> 01:53:57.297
Where it's like, anime-loving otakus

01:53:57.297 --> 01:53:58.940
are like, "Ooh, sweaty, gross."

01:53:58.940 --> 01:54:00.660
And then meanwhile, if
there's a guy rocking up

01:54:00.660 --> 01:54:03.047
with like full on like,
you know, idols shit,

01:54:03.047 --> 01:54:05.266
they're like, "Yeah, he
just really likes idols."

01:54:05.266 --> 01:54:07.493
It's like, no, he's just a sweaty.

01:54:08.380 --> 01:54:09.830
- A lot of people think it's gross.

01:54:09.830 --> 01:54:11.863
Every time I told Japanese people,

01:54:11.863 --> 01:54:13.650
like, "Oh, I'm an otaku,"

01:54:13.650 --> 01:54:16.460
they're like, "No, you're not,
you don't look like otaku."

01:54:16.460 --> 01:54:18.369
What does otaku look like?

01:54:18.369 --> 01:54:20.983
And it's like, really, how
dare you insult my people?

01:54:22.600 --> 01:54:26.260
- Yeah, like what do they
expect an otaku to look like?

01:54:26.260 --> 01:54:28.410
Where it's like, you're
fuckin' 300 pounds.

01:54:29.494 --> 01:54:31.810
- I think it's like how the word weeaboo

01:54:31.810 --> 01:54:34.450
used to be seen like 10
years ago in our community

01:54:34.450 --> 01:54:38.020
where, who would identify
themselves as a weeaboo?

01:54:38.020 --> 01:54:42.710
That's like a cringy person
nobody wants to know, right?

01:54:42.710 --> 01:54:45.840
- Because the stereotypical
otaku in Japan,

01:54:45.840 --> 01:54:47.560
you don't see people like that anymore.

01:54:47.560 --> 01:54:48.903
There's people who like anime

01:54:48.903 --> 01:54:51.190
and that's just normalized here.

01:54:51.190 --> 01:54:52.023
- That was cool.

01:54:52.023 --> 01:54:54.810
Like 20 year old girls
have like the Ita-bags,

01:54:54.810 --> 01:54:57.027
and love 2d boys.

01:54:57.027 --> 01:54:59.473
- And everyone loves JoJo's.

01:55:02.322 --> 01:55:03.477
That's like the Chi-Chi, right?

01:55:04.329 --> 01:55:06.530
Where you just where JoJo
merch to a business meeting-

01:55:06.530 --> 01:55:08.991
- It just feels so good.

01:55:08.991 --> 01:55:10.330
- You'll get like a 50
year old businessman

01:55:10.330 --> 01:55:12.027
and be like, I love JoJo's.

01:55:13.600 --> 01:55:15.910
- I was buying manga,
like my local bookstore,

01:55:15.910 --> 01:55:17.866
and I was wearing one
of the glam JoJo shirts,

01:55:17.866 --> 01:55:20.411
and a guy stopped putting in the books

01:55:20.411 --> 01:55:23.078
and he's like, "Where'd
you get that shirt from?"

01:55:23.078 --> 01:55:23.911
And I was like, "Oh, glam."

01:55:23.911 --> 01:55:25.520
and he's like, "That looks sick, dude."

01:55:25.520 --> 01:55:26.883
He just high fived me, I'm like,

01:55:26.883 --> 01:55:28.883
I've never had this experience in Japan.

01:55:30.610 --> 01:55:33.410
Obviously this was before the
pandemic, social distance.

01:55:34.552 --> 01:55:36.417
- And everyone clapped. (laughing)

01:55:36.417 --> 01:55:39.227
- And everyone in the
store applauded Joey.

01:55:39.227 --> 01:55:42.269
- And then they played the
JoJo opening and I left.

01:55:42.269 --> 01:55:46.574
- JoJo's theme came on.
- As I run out the store.

01:55:46.574 --> 01:55:47.680
Yeah, he just straight up high-fived me,

01:55:47.680 --> 01:55:49.420
I was like, cool.

01:55:49.420 --> 01:55:52.220
Jojo brings people together
man, that's fucking awesome.

01:55:53.782 --> 01:55:55.430
- I remember I mentioned in my,

01:55:55.430 --> 01:55:57.010
like when I streamed the
dating in Japan video,

01:55:57.010 --> 01:55:57.983
I mentioned like, oh yeah,

01:55:57.983 --> 01:55:59.640
I just openly said I was an otaku,

01:55:59.640 --> 01:56:00.473
and then I was like,

01:56:00.473 --> 01:56:01.800
why are people getting
all worked up about it?

01:56:01.800 --> 01:56:02.660
And then I've got loads of comments

01:56:02.660 --> 01:56:05.336
they're like, "It means
a bad thing in Japan."

01:56:05.336 --> 01:56:07.640
I was like, yeah, I
know, I just don't care.

01:56:07.640 --> 01:56:10.540
If I can normalize it, I
can help to get there, sure.

01:56:10.540 --> 01:56:11.810
- Someone wants to see
it in a negative light,

01:56:11.810 --> 01:56:13.060
that's their prerogative.

01:56:14.150 --> 01:56:14.983
- Most of the people that I told it to

01:56:14.983 --> 01:56:16.170
just didn't give a shit.

01:56:16.170 --> 01:56:17.310
- Going back to conventions though,

01:56:17.310 --> 01:56:18.340
one thing I've always been curious about

01:56:18.340 --> 01:56:22.110
is how would you guys pick
the guests to bring over

01:56:22.110 --> 01:56:23.490
and showcase to people?

01:56:23.490 --> 01:56:24.800
Is there a process behind that or?

01:56:24.800 --> 01:56:27.470
- Yeah, for my company, there was,

01:56:27.470 --> 01:56:32.470
at the time when I was like
the person in charge of events.

01:56:32.690 --> 01:56:36.400
We would look first at the
sales, like, oh, what's trending,

01:56:36.400 --> 01:56:39.180
what's like the top selling manga

01:56:39.180 --> 01:56:40.810
or light novels, the top 10,

01:56:40.810 --> 01:56:45.060
and then I just contact them, yeah.

01:56:45.060 --> 01:56:47.410
And because I'm in Book Walker,

01:56:47.410 --> 01:56:49.860
and Book Walker like has really close ties

01:56:49.860 --> 01:56:51.710
to all different kinds of publishers,

01:56:53.270 --> 01:56:55.193
I guess we're able to ask them.

01:56:57.385 --> 01:57:01.070
- That makes sense, 'cause I've
done so many creative panels

01:57:01.070 --> 01:57:02.810
especially at AX with Book Walker.

01:57:02.810 --> 01:57:07.158
- Like "DanMachi",
"Overlord","Goblin Slayer",

01:57:07.158 --> 01:57:08.008
"Monster Musume".

01:57:09.103 --> 01:57:13.240
- And I remember every time
one of those panels finish,

01:57:13.240 --> 01:57:15.820
it's always one person who
comes up to me for some reason

01:57:15.820 --> 01:57:17.037
of all people and it's like,

01:57:17.037 --> 01:57:21.720
"Hey, next year can you get
the author of this light novel?

01:57:21.720 --> 01:57:22.553
That'd be really cool."

01:57:22.553 --> 01:57:23.840
- Did you ever get people who thought

01:57:23.840 --> 01:57:25.190
you were the author of something?

01:57:25.190 --> 01:57:26.910
- Yeah.
- I've had that as well.

01:57:27.810 --> 01:57:29.936
Someone came up to my thing,

01:57:29.936 --> 01:57:32.193
this is back when the
93% was still a thing.

01:57:33.510 --> 01:57:36.007
Some guy, like a 13 year
old boy came up to me,

01:57:36.007 --> 01:57:36.840
and I'm just like, that's weird,

01:57:36.840 --> 01:57:38.260
why is there a guy in my line?

01:57:39.771 --> 01:57:41.670
- The 7%?

01:57:41.670 --> 01:57:43.520
- He comes up to me and
he's like, oh yeah, yeah.

01:57:43.520 --> 01:57:44.990
He took a photo, did the signing,

01:57:44.990 --> 01:57:47.583
he's like, you're the author
of "Goblin Slayer", right?

01:57:49.060 --> 01:57:50.470
No kidding.

01:57:50.470 --> 01:57:51.533
I was like, "No."

01:57:51.533 --> 01:57:53.607
- Because we had autographs at our booth

01:57:53.607 --> 01:57:55.140
for "Goblin Slayer".

01:57:55.140 --> 01:57:56.299
- He was like, "You're the
author of 'Goblin Slayer'."

01:57:56.299 --> 01:57:58.854
I'm just like, do I look like Japanese?

01:57:58.854 --> 01:58:01.180
- I don't get a lot of,
"Oh, you're the author,"

01:58:01.180 --> 01:58:03.210
but I always get a lot
of like, "You're the guy

01:58:03.210 --> 01:58:05.611
who does the official translation."

01:58:05.611 --> 01:58:09.083
It's like because you translated
what the author was saying,

01:58:09.083 --> 01:58:09.916
so you must split...

01:58:09.916 --> 01:58:12.410
And yeah, I remember I
had a guy just being like,

01:58:12.410 --> 01:58:15.600
when's volume three of "Goblin
Slayer" gonna get translated?

01:58:15.600 --> 01:58:16.703
Like I fuckin' know.

01:58:18.600 --> 01:58:20.080
- You should've just been
like, yeah actually tomorrow.

01:58:20.080 --> 01:58:22.280
- I'm working on it right
now actually, don't worry,

01:58:22.280 --> 01:58:23.113
it's coming soon.

01:58:23.113 --> 01:58:25.688
- If you would've just left me
alone, it would've been done.

01:58:25.688 --> 01:58:27.127
- Yeah, I get so many
people just being like,

01:58:27.127 --> 01:58:29.150
"Hey, can you bring on this creator?

01:58:29.150 --> 01:58:30.260
Or like this author?"

01:58:30.260 --> 01:58:33.427
I'm just like, don't ask me
about it, I'm just hosting it.

01:58:33.427 --> 01:58:36.070
- The anime departments
of certain companies,

01:58:36.070 --> 01:58:41.070
they usually bring guests of
like say directors or whatever

01:58:41.100 --> 01:58:43.260
of whatever anime is
coming out that season,

01:58:43.260 --> 01:58:44.320
and they have a premiere

01:58:44.320 --> 01:58:46.950
and the author or director
comes to promote the premiere.

01:58:46.950 --> 01:58:49.610
So not necessarily, it's like trending-

01:58:49.610 --> 01:58:52.610
- So it's basically
just all marketing then,

01:58:52.610 --> 01:58:53.460
is the way I see it.

01:58:53.460 --> 01:58:55.950
It's all data, it's all marketing.

01:58:55.950 --> 01:58:57.867
- It's all data and marketing,
it's all businesses.

01:58:57.867 --> 01:58:59.070
- 'Cause I always wondered,

01:58:59.070 --> 01:59:00.500
AX must be really expensive

01:59:00.500 --> 01:59:04.370
to like put up all the booth,
the staff, the marketing.

01:59:04.370 --> 01:59:06.900
Is it like, I mean, it obviously
it must be worth it, right?

01:59:06.900 --> 01:59:09.012
Like, is the return on
investment huge then?

01:59:09.012 --> 01:59:12.688
- I mean, they're not really
looking for sales directly.

01:59:12.688 --> 01:59:14.479
- I think it's just looking for presence.

01:59:14.479 --> 01:59:15.890
- Brand presence.
- If you don't have presence,

01:59:15.890 --> 01:59:17.810
it's like not being at
E3, you know what I mean?

01:59:17.810 --> 01:59:19.380
- Guess even companies now are questioning

01:59:19.380 --> 01:59:20.935
if E3's worth it.

01:59:20.935 --> 01:59:24.757
- I mean, Sony wasn't there
E3 the year before I think,

01:59:24.757 --> 01:59:25.990
and that was like massive news

01:59:25.990 --> 01:59:28.020
because that was like a massive statement

01:59:28.020 --> 01:59:30.760
being like, "Hey, we
don't fuckin' need E3."

01:59:30.760 --> 01:59:32.670
- We're fuckin' Sony.

01:59:32.670 --> 01:59:34.270
- I wonder if that would
be a thing with that.

01:59:34.270 --> 01:59:35.220
Like AX when the company is like,

01:59:35.220 --> 01:59:37.040
Oh we don't really need AX anymore.

01:59:37.040 --> 01:59:39.400
- A lot of companies
though, Japanese companies

01:59:39.400 --> 01:59:41.060
are bringing their own guests

01:59:41.060 --> 01:59:44.420
instead of doing the guest of honor thing.

01:59:44.420 --> 01:59:45.481
- 'Cause I feel like right now

01:59:45.481 --> 01:59:48.360
with like anime growing
so much in the West,

01:59:48.360 --> 01:59:53.360
I think there is a big, like
there's more eyes at AX now,

01:59:54.360 --> 01:59:55.730
so like, if you're not there,

01:59:55.730 --> 01:59:59.367
then that's like a missed
opportunity more than,

01:59:59.367 --> 02:00:01.845
"Hey, this is E3, this
is already established."

02:00:01.845 --> 02:00:03.310
- There's so much press too,

02:00:03.310 --> 02:00:05.610
a lot of news sites are all at this events

02:00:05.610 --> 02:00:07.690
so they could arrange interviews

02:00:07.690 --> 02:00:09.200
or whatever that could promote it.

02:00:09.200 --> 02:00:12.563
- So going back 900 million topics ago,

